            <ArticleTitle>Fetiform Teratoma in the Ovary of a 7-Year-Old Girl: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>256-258</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1083-3188(16)30229-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpag.2016.10.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Fetiform teratoma, a highly differentiated mature cystic teratoma resembling a fetus, is rare and typically found in the ovaries of women of reproductive age. In this report we describe, to our knowledge, the youngest case of ovarian fetiform teratoma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 7-year-old girl presented with acute abdominal pain. Radiological examinations revealed a 5.2-cm ovarian complex cystic mass with fetal-like components in favor of fetus in fetu and teratoma. After surgical removal, the mass resembled a fetus consisting of a head, two eye slits, two small upper limb projections, and hair. Pathology indicated mature cystic teratoma supporting the diagnosis of fetiform teratoma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although not commonly found in children, fetiform teratoma must be considered in the diagnosis of a child who presents with an adnexal mass resembling a fetus.</AbstractText>
